However, the problem statement says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same."
This means we are counting pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is on top of $A_j$ and $2 A_i \le A_j$.
Since the order matters (top vs bottom), $(i, j)$ is distinct from $(j, i)$.
But the condition $2 A_i \le A_j$ implies $A_i \le A_j/2$. Since $A_i \ge 1$, this implies $A_i < A_j$ (unless $A_j=0$ which is not allowed, or $A_i$ is very small). Actually, since $A_i \ge 1$, $2 A_i \ge 2$, so $A_j \ge 2$. Also $A_i \le A_j/2 < A_j$. So $A_i$ must be strictly smaller than $A_j$.
Thus, if $A_i$ is on top of $A_j$, then $A_i < A_j$.
Since the array $A$ is sorted, if we pick two indices $i$ and $j$ with $i < j$, it is possible that $A_i$ is on top of $A_j$. It is impossible for $A_j$ to be on top of $A_i$ because $A_j \ge A_i$ and the condition requires the top one to be at most half the bottom one. If $A_j$ is on top of $A_i$, then $A_j \le A_i/2$, which implies $A_j < A_i$, a contradiction.
So we only need to consider pairs $(i, j)$ such that $i < j$ and $2 A_i \le A_j$.

